n this business but the Iraqi  women have actually moved those girls  out because they're cheaper and so in a  way Syria private business does benefit  from these women and you know what  country actually makes such a crackdown  on prostitution it happens all over the  world let's go back up to some of the  regional consequences of this crisis you  described in the book may of 2008 in  Lebanon where Hezbollah the Shia militia  and in Lebanon basically took over West  Beirut the the largely Sunni part of  Beirut and this was something that was  witnessed and it was something that was  documented and everyone sort of in the  entire Middle East was on edge  seeing the Shiite force exert its power  within Lebanon in this way  how much did Iraq have to do with that  everything well that probably isn't  completely fair because it would assume  that the Lebanese don't have their own  national interests but so much of it did  have to do with Iraq as as I've said in  the earlier parts of this interview  there were plenty of young men in in  northern Lebanon and in eastern Lebanon  who decided that they were going to go  fight in Iraq and and what they saw is  they were fighting against the Americans  but they were also fighting against an  american-backed Shia Arab government  something that that was a historic  change in the Middle East now as we know  Hezbollah is an ally of Iran and if you  look at the geopolitical importance of  Iraq this is where we have uncomfortable  competition between the Americans and  the Iranians so what we were seeing in  Lebanon in 2008 was essentially the same  conflict carried out by different actors  yes they have their own interest on the  ground but it is also part of this  regional competition for who actually  has the most influence in the region and  I think that what was interesting about  the outcome in 2008  set the stage for what has happened ever  since the truth of the matter is that  Hezbollah Iran and Syria won that round  wheth