viously this eclipse  of the Sunnis was was one of the  unintended consequences what else have  you seen and documented you know on the  ground in Iraq it became apparent  somewhere around 2004 when we first  arrived we didn't ask anybody who came  to work for us what they were it's it's  very American not to ask but it was very  clear when we would take translators out  to different neighborhoods it we learned  that you couldn't take a Shiite into a  Sunni neighborhood or a Sunni into a  Shia neighborhood so there's all that  grassroots divide that you feel in Iraq  but still there today most of the  neighbourhoods have become unmixed  before 2003 Sunnis and Shias would live  in the same neighborhoods now that is  most that is less likely let's remember  it was the Jordanian king who spoke  early on about something that he called  the Shia Crescent and what he was  talking about is he is a man who rules a  predominantly Sunni country that's  Jordan and he was worried about the  rising power of Iran that what the  invasion had done was to take Saddam out  of the equation and that was Iran's  biggest enemy and the Jordanian King  said it out loud  other Arab leaders felt it acutely but  didn't say it out loud that this was a  moment that Iran had what had become a  more powerful force in the country so  that is the geopolitical sunni-shia  divide and where you see this played out  is certainly in Lebanon you see it  played out in Syria in Saudi Arabia and  Egypt in all the places where you have  Sunni Shia populations you know it is  played out in different ways but as I  said earlier this is a political divide  not a religious divide this is not  people who are fighting in the mosques  this is this is about how politics and  power are going to be realigned in the  region and nobody really has an answer  to that yet and these you mentioned some  of the surrounding countries you  Jordan and Lebanon and Syria and Saudi  Arabia one of the things you touch on  and in the cases of Jordan and Lebanon  you write about this in great detail is  the impact that Iraq has had on those  countries but also what some of those  countries and what some of those regimes  and at least are doing inside Iraq  especially Jordan and Saudi Arabia and  Iran do you see any possibility for  stable Iraq with the pressure that a lot  of these countries exert um it depends  on how strong Iraq comes out of this  election but there's here's a couple of  examples the Saudi Arabian King has  never actually met with the Iraqi Prime  Minister who is a Shiite Saudi Arabia is  a predominantly Sunni country it they  have not put an ambassador in other  other Arab countries have done so but  the Saudis have resisted putting an  ambassador into Iraq and it's unclear to  me what that relationship is going to be  when Saddam was in power the Saudis and  Iraq actually were quite close and  fought on the same side in the iran-iraq  war and so that relationship will will  evolve over time where you really see  the divide is in Lebanon very early on  there were Sunnis in Lebanon who joined  the jihadist insurgents whatever you  want to call them inside of Iraq and  they felt that it was their place to go  and fight and what they were fighting  for was Sunni hegemony in the region not  again not in the mosque but as a  political battle and there were many of  them who went to Iraq who were trained  in urban warfare and then came home and  as you know this actually created a  crisis in Lebanon there was a group  called Fattah el Aslam these were people  who had come back from Iraq who had gone  through Syria into northern Lebanon and  you know there was a clash in the  country over this I spoke to a Sunni  Imam whose job it was to bring these  young men who now were in jail  them back into a non-violent way to  express their their views but you know  the thing that he noted about all of the  people who are on the Terrorism wing in  a Lebanese prison is they were all  Sunnis and this bothered him greatly how  is it possible that it's only us that  are considered terrorists on our in our  own country and so those tensions are  there how this plays in Iraq these  things take time  Iraq is certainly not a stable country  today as I say about the election that  has just passed you have democracy in  Iraq but without Democrats it's  interesting when you recount the  experience in Lebanon and some of the  other places in the region has Iraq  become something of Afghanistan in the  1980s where there was this great jihad  against the Soviet occupation of  Afghanistan in the 1980s a lot of young  men young mostly Sunni Arab fighters  would get trained sure four years in  Afghanistan and then there would be this  effective blowback they would go back to  their home countries places like Egypt  places like Saudi Arabia do you did you  find that pattern in Iraq as this place  for jihad and then these young men would  return to these countries and the impact  in countries other than just Lebanon you  know it's a little bit different than  Afghanistan there has been one occasion  Fatah al-islam and this is a group of  men who came to northern Lebanon and I  think you can explain that in that it is  easy for them to get across those  borders to go from Iraq to Syria to  Lebanon it is not so easy this time with  Interpol remember how many American  soldiers were in Iraq and they were very  very diligent in you know taking names  doing iris scans they have an amazing  amount of data this time that did not  happen in Afghanistan so I think it's  going to be a little harder than it was  and the model is an interesting one to  watch whether these young men who've now  been trained in urban warfare will be  able to seep back into their countries  and cause the kind of trouble that they  were able to do after Afghanistan I  think the model is going to be a little  bit different simply because many many  states have seen it before they expect  it to come and t