y want support but the  question for these refugees and what  have you done for us nothing nothing at  all and you're part of this government  structure you're part of this permanent  political class that rules Baghdad and I  suppose that what we should say instead  is 42,000 came out to vote these are  people who aren't living a great life in  exile and so you still have 42,000  people who are invested in the country I  go back to a number there are 230  thousand refugees who are registered  with UNH see we've got and it doesn't  represent everybody there those are the  people who are interested in being  resettled the rest of them whatever  their numbers are want to go home so the  fact that we had 42,000 vote tells you  that at least in in March of 2010 that  there were that many people who are  still invested in Iraq now if this  government that is about to form if this  government does not find a way to either  support them or bring them back then my  guess is there won't be a single one who  votes next time and these refugees are  going to watch very closely the  formation of the next government yes  that's something that could take four or  five six months as all the different  factions finally when they finally get  their allotment of seats no one is going  to have a clear majority there's going  to be a lot of backroom dealing over the  Prime Minister spot and also over the  presidency so what will the refugees be  watching for in that backroom dealing  and is there any way that they can get  they didn't vote in large numbers so is  there any way that they can get some of  their issues on the table maybe more aid  to some of the surrounding countries you  know mr. Lau II who is now the head of  strangely the head of this Sunni bloc  has spoken about helping exiles he's  he's an Hashim II who was a president  and will and and and aligned himself  with allow he also has been a proponent  of doing something about the exiles so  let's see what they do when they